Q2. Transaction?
Ans:
A Transaction is a group of operations that follow ACID properties.

What @Transactional does?
When you add 

@Service
public class BankService {
	@Transactional
	public void tranferMoney() {
		withdraw();
		deposit();	
	}
}


Spring ensures:
Both method succeeds --> commit
Any exception --> rollback

How it works internally??
1. Spring Creates a proxy
Spring doesn't modify your class directly.
It creates proxy around your class
So when you call the method, you are actually calling the proxy, not the original method.

2. Transaction Starts Before Method
Proxy intercepts the call
Client -> Proxy -> Actual Method


3. Method Executes
Your business logic runs normally
withdraw();
deposit();

4. Commit or Rollback
If everything is fine COMMIT
If Exception occurs ROLLBACK 


Important Rules:
1. Only works on public methods
2. Self invocation problem 

3. Rollback rules
By default:
- Rolls back on RuntimeException
- Not on checked exception
You can change it 
@Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class)

4. Propagation (Advanced)
Controls how transactions behave when one calls another
REQUIRED (default), REQUIRES_NEW, MANDATORY etc
